<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.3.4: Dividing Polynomial Functions and the Remainder Theorem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01620</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.3.4: Dividing Polynomial Functions and the Remainder Theorem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9797dc54-9f7d-41f7-b250-34eeb66e11db</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Activity</title>
    <para id="para-00001">Divide the polynomial functions. Find the quotient value.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00002">\(f(x)=x^2-5x-24\) 
        \(g(x)=x+3\)
        
        \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
        
        \(f(-3) =\) ___________
    </para>
    <para id="para-00003">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00005">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-8\)
\(f(-3)=0\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    
    <para id="para-00006">\(f(x)=x^2-15x+54\)
        \(g(x)=x-9\)
        
        \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
        
        \(f(9) =\) ___________
    </para>
    <para id="para-00007">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
  <para id="para-00008">Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00009">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-6\)
\(f(9)=0\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00003">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    
    <para id="para-00010">\(f(x)=x^2+2x-3\)
        \(g(x)=x+3\)
        
        \((\frac{f}{g})(x) =\) ___________
        
        \(f(-3) =\) ___________
    </para>
    <para id="para-00011">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
  <para id="para-00012">Compare your answers:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00013">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-1\)
\(f(-3)=0\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para id="para-00014">The Remainder Theorem states that if a polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\). This means we can always compare the remainder by finding \(f(c)\) when the divisor is written in the form \(x-c\).</para>

<exercise id="_1354241e-b60c-45b8-951b-099be0a8eba8" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00015">Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3-7x+12\) is divided by \(x + 3\).</para>
        <para id="para-00016">Enter the remainder here.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00017">Correct! The remainder is 6</para><para id="para-00018">Nice try! The correct answer is: 6</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_53f4a375-e2b9-4945-a16f-9fc9e4fd3361" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00019">Enter the remainder here.</para><para id="para-00020">Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=2x^3-6x-24\) is divided by \(x - 3\).</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00021">Correct! The remainder is 12</para><para id="para-00022">Nice try! The correct answer is: 12</para></solution></exercise></section>

<note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><para id="para-00023">For functions \(f(x)=x^3+2x^2-19x+12\) and \(g(x)=x-3\):</para>
        <para id="para-00024">Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\) and \((\frac{f}{g})(0)\).</para></note>
        
<section id="sect-00002">
  <title>Additional Resources</title>
 
    <para id="para-00025">In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
    <para id="para-00026">Just as polynomials can be divided, polynomial functions can also be divided.</para>
    
      <para id="para-00027">
       <emphasis> DIVISION OF POLYNOMIAL FUNCTIONS</emphasis> 
    </para>
      
      <para id="para-00028">For functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), where \(g(x) \neq 0\),</para>
      <para id="para-00029">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)</para>
    
    <para id="para-00030"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
    <para id="para-00031">For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-14\) and \(g(x)=x+2\):</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
      <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)  Substitute for \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\).</para>
    <para id="para-00033">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)=x^2-5x-14}{x+2}\)</para>
    <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Divide the polynomials using long division.</para>
    <para id="para-00035">\[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,x - 7\\x + 2\left){\vphantom{1{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\right.
\!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({x^2} + 2x)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, - 7x - 14\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ( - 7x - 14)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]
\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-7\)</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
      <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\).</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00036"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> In part (1), we found \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</para>
    <para id="para-00037">\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x−7\)</para>
    <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> To find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\), substitute \(x=-4\).</para>
    <para id="para-00039">\((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-4-7\)
\((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-11\)</para>
    <note class="try" id="note-00002">
<title>Try It: Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
    
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00040">For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-36\) and \(g(x)=x+4\):</para>
          <para id="para-00041">Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</para>
          <para id="para-00042">Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-5)\).</para>
          <para id="para-00043">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00044">Here is how to solve these polynomial function division problems:</para>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
            <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(x) = \frac{x^2-5x-36}{x+4}=x-9\)</item>
          </list>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
            <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(-5)=x-9=(-5)-9=-14\)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise></note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">  <title>Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
    <para id="para-00045">Let’s look at some division problems and their remainders. They are summarized in the chart below. If we take the dividend from each division problem and use it to define a function, we get the functions shown in the chart. When the divisor is written as \(x - c\), the value of the function at \(c\), \(f(c)\), is the same as the remainder from the division problem.</para>
    <table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable" id="table-00001">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry>
           <emphasis>Dividend</emphasis>
          </entry>
          <entry>
           <emphasis>Divisor</emphasis>
          </entry>
          <entry>
           <emphasis>Remainder</emphasis>
          </entry>
          <entry>
           <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
          </entry>
          <entry>
           <emphasis>\(f(c)\)</emphasis>
          </entry>
        </row>
      <row>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00046">\(x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00047">\(x - (-3)\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00048">\(3\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00049">\(f(x)=x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00050">\(f(-3)=3\)</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00051">\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00052">\(x - (-3)\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00053">\(-61\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00054">\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00055">\(f(-3)=-61\)</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00056">\(x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00057">\(x - (-4)\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00058">\(3\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00059">\(f(x)=x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00060">\(f(-4)=3\)</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    <para id="para-00061">To see this more generally, we realize we can check a division problem by multiplying the quotient times the divisor and adding the remainder. In function notation, we could say: To get the dividend \(f(x)\), we multiply the quotient, \(q(x)\), times the divisor, \(x-c\), and add the remainder, \(r\).</para>
    <para id="para-00062">\(f(x)=q(x)(x-c)+r\)</para>
    <para id="para-00063">If we evaluate this at \(c\), we get:
\(f({\style{color:red}c})=q({\style{color:red}c})({\style{color:red}c}-c)+r\)
\(f(c)=q(c)(0)+r\)
\(f(c)=r\)</para>
    <para id="para-00064">This leads us to the Remainder Theorem.</para>
    <note id="note-00003">
      <para id="para-00065">
       <emphasis>REMAINDER THEOREM</emphasis>
    </para>
      
      <para id="para-00066">If the polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\).</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
    <para id="para-00068">Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
    <para id="para-00069">To use the Remainder Theorem, we must use the divisor in the \(x-c\) form. We can write the divisor \(x+2\) as \(x-(-2)\). So, our \(c\) is \(-2\).</para>
    <para id="para-00070">To find the remainder, we evaluate \(f(c)\), which is \(f(-2)\).</para>
    <para id="para-00071">\(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\)</para>
    <para id="para-00072"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> To evaluate \(f(-2)\), substitute \(x=-2\).</para>
    <para id="para-00073">\(f({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})=({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})^3+3({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})+19\)</para>
    <para id="para-00074"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify.</para>
    <para id="para-00075">\(f(-2)=(-8)-6+19\) 
\(f(-2)=5\)
The remainder is 5 when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x+2\).</para>
    <para id="para-00076"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check using synthetic division.</para>
    <para id="para-00077">
      
        <media alt=".">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_06_11_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      
  </para>
    <note class="try" id="note-00004">
<title>Try It: Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
    
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00078">Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
          <para id="para-00079">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00080">Here is how to solve a problem using the Remainder Theorem:</para>
          <para id="para-00081">\(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\)</para>
          <para id="para-00082">\(f(2)=(-2)^3+4(-2)+15\)</para>
          <para id="para-00083">\(f(2)=-8-8+15= -1\)</para>
          <para id="para-00084">The remainder is \(-1\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    </section> 
</section>
</content>
